        "title": "Renaissance and Baroque Architecture",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The Renaissance is first a structural problem solved (Florence Cathedral dome, 1436)\nand then a theoretical project (Vitruvius rediscovered, proportion systematized). The\nBaroque is what happens when the theory is fully internalized and the goal shifts from\nrational order to emotional effect. Both treat the building as a composed object — the\nresult of a conscious design process — rather than an accumulated construction."
    },
